---
import Pattern from "../../layouts/Pattern.astro";
import PaperHeader from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/PaperHeader.astro";
import Abstract from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Abstract.astro";
import Equation from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Equation.astro";
import M from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/M.astro";
import Env from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Env.astro";
import Cite from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Cite.astro";
import BibTeX from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/BibTeX.astro";
import Sidenote from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Sidenote.astro";
import LineChartExample from "../../components/LineChartExample.astro";
---
<Pattern title="Paper page" current="Publications" description="An example paper page built with Polytope: a header with authors and links, figures, sidenotes, citations, BibTeX, and references.">
  <div class="ply-container">
    <PaperHeader
      venue="Example Venue 2026"
      title="Example title: higher-order message passing on cell complexes"
      authors={[{ name: "A. Author", affiliations: [1], equal: true }, { name: "John Doe", affiliations: [2], equal: true }, { name: "B. Author", affiliations: [1, 2] }]}
      affiliations={["Example University", "Another Institute"]}
      links={[{ label: "Paper", href: "#", icon: "paper" }, { label: "Code", href: "#", icon: "code" }, { label: "BibTeX", href: "#cite", icon: "quote" }]}
    />
  </div>
  <hr class="ply-rule" />
  <div class="ply-container pp">
    <div class="pp__main" data-toc-scope>
      <div class="pp__figure"><LineChartExample legend={false} /></div>
      <div class="ply-prose ply-has-sidenotes">
        <Abstract>
          <p>Example abstract. Message passing networks on graphs aggregate information over pairs of nodes. We extend the aggregation to cells of every dimension through the boundary maps of a cell complex, and show that the resulting layers are as expressive as the cellular Weisfeiler-Lehman test. On three synthetic benchmarks the model improves accuracy at a similar parameter count.</p>
        </Abstract>

        <h2>1. Introduction</h2>
        <p>Graph neural networks pass messages along edges<Cite n={1} title="Example reference: neural message passing" authors="E. Author, F. Author" venue="Example Journal" year={2017} />. Cell complexes add faces of higher dimension, so a layer can also pass messages between edges that bound the same face.<Sidenote n={1}>A sidenote sits next to the sentence it explains, in the margin on wide screens.</Sidenote></p>

        <h2>2. Method</h2>
        <Env kind="definition" num="2.1" name="Boundary matrix">
          For a cell complex <M tex={String.raw`X`} />, the boundary matrix <M tex={String.raw`B_k`} /> has one row per <M tex={String.raw`(k-1)`} />-cell and one column per <M tex={String.raw`k`} />-cell, with entry <M tex={String.raw`\pm 1`} /> when the row cell lies on the boundary of the column cell.
        </Env>
        <p>A layer updates the features <M tex={String.raw`H_k`} /> on <M tex={String.raw`k`} />-cells from both neighboring dimensions:</p>
        <Equation tex={String.raw`H_k' = \sigma\left( B_k^{\top} H_{k-1} W_{\downarrow} + H_k W + B_{k+1} H_{k+1} W_{\uparrow} \right)`} num={1} />
        <Env kind="theorem" num="2.2">With injective aggregation, the layer in equation (1) distinguishes any two cell complexes that the cellular Weisfeiler-Lehman test distinguishes.</Env>
        <Env kind="proof">Example proof sketch. Replace with the argument from the paper.</Env>

        <h2>3. Results</h2>
        <div class="ply-table-wrap">
          <table class="ply-table">
            <caption>Synthetic example. Test accuracy, mean of five seeds.</caption>
            <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th class="ply-num">Task 1</th><th class="ply-num">Task 2</th><th class="ply-num">Task 3</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Model A</td><td class="ply-num"><strong>0.861</strong></td><td class="ply-num"><strong>0.774</strong></td><td class="ply-num">0.912</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Model B</td><td class="ply-num">0.809</td><td class="ply-num">0.751</td><td class="ply-num"><strong>0.918</strong></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Model C</td><td class="ply-num">0.740</td><td class="ply-num">0.702</td><td class="ply-num">0.880</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2 id="cite">Cite</h2>
        <BibTeX entry={`@inproceedings{author2026example,
  title     = {Example title: higher-order message passing on cell complexes},
  author    = {Author, A. and Doe, John and Author, B.},
  booktitle = {Example Venue},
  year      = {2026}
}`} />

        <h2>References</h2>
        <ol class="ply-footnotes pp__refs">
          <li id="ref-1">E. Author, F. Author. Example reference: neural message passing. Example Journal, 2017.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="pp__toc"><nav class="ply-toc" data-auto aria-label="On this page"><p class="ply-toc__heading">On this page</p><ul></ul></nav></div>
  </div>
</Pattern>
<style>
  .pp { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--ply-space-7); padding-top: var(--ply-space-6); }
  .pp__main { min-width: 0; container: ply-main / inline-size; }
  .pp__toc { display: none; }
  @media (min-width: 80rem) { .pp { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) var(--ply-layout-toc); } .pp__toc { display: block; position: sticky; top: var(--ply-space-5); align-self: start; } }
  .pp__figure { max-width: 52rem; margin-bottom: var(--ply-space-7); }
  .pp__refs { margin-top: var(--ply-space-3); border-top: 0; padding-top: 0; }
</style>
